<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Britons (Celtic people)</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ancient Britons</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Britons were the Celtic-speaking inhabitants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> through the Roman period and the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. They spoke Brittonic languages, the ancestors of Welsh, Cornish and Breton, and were organised in peoples such as the Catuvellauni and Iceni. After the Roman withdrawal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> settlement, Britons kept independent kingdoms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornwall">Cornwall</a> and the north, and some migrated to Brittany.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/britons-celtic-people.jpg" alt="Britons (Celtic people)" width="220" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: malus36 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Gaels_Brythons_Picts_GB.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
